                  \\Big)
                  \\,.
          %p
            By the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+isomorphism"} de Rham isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            (regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete
            topological group).
          %p
            Combined with the cohomology operation induced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+scalars"} extension of scalars
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi n
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^n \mathbb{Z} \xrightarrow{\;} B^n \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
            %em ordinary character map
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/OrdinaryCharacterMap.jpg", :width => "520px"}/
          %p
            Beware that this character map is not in general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+function"} injective
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+subgroups"} torsion subgroups
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+cohomology"} integral cohomology
            group. Nevertheless — or rather: therefore! — the ordinary character map provides the
            %em first approximation
            to integral cohomology, which is generally more readily computed than the full integral cohomology.
          %p
            In consequence, when applied to integral characteristic classes then the ordinary character gives a useful first approximation to ordinary non-abelian cohomology. Specifically for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+group"} unitary
            principal bundles we obtain a sequence of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em Chern-de Rham classes
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ChernDeRhamClasses.jpg", :width => "750px"}/
          %p But since de Rham cohomology is a local differential-geometric notion, the question arises:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "center", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi Is
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi it
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi possible
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi to
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi construct
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi the
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi Chern
                        %mtext -
                        %mi de
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi Rham
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi classes
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi directly
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi by
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi differential
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi geometry
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi on
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi principal
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi bundles
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %mi without
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi going
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi through
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi these
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi topological
                        %mi constructions
                        %mo ?
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{c}
                  Is \; it \; possible \; to \; construct \; the \; Chern\text{-}de \; Rham \; classes
                  \\\
                  directly \; by \; differential \; geometry \; on \; principal \; bundles
                  \\\
                  without \; going \; through \; these \; topological constructions?
                  \\end{array}
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} Connections
            are the answer to this question.
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#OrdinaryConnections Ordinary Connections
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} functor assigning
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+valued+differential+forms"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
              \-valued differential forms
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} dR
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ;
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %msub
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
